#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""dsh-serve — start and stop the DSH GPU tenant under ARM arbitration.

The GPU tenant of the local agent stack is the vLLM server holding
Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4 (~25 GB, weight load until process exit), not the
`deepseek-harness` repo, which is an HTTP client of it and touches no GPU. That
repo is an upstream-clean clone and carries no ARM code, so the integration
lives here: one launcher, outside any git tree, in the shape of `~/bin/arm` and
`~/bin/aivan-indexer`.

Mode is WRAPPED (ARM consumer brief §4a): ARM holds the lease for the child's
lifetime. App `dsh`, pool `gpu0`, always `--exclusive`, registered default
priority `fleet` — fleet substrate serving a dev harness, which must queue
behind `inference` and `vl-server` because production depends on those.

⛔ The lease must never outlive the residency.

`arm run` blocks on `child.wait()` and releases the lease in the `finally` that
follows, so it releases when its DIRECT child exits. vLLM forks
`VLLM::EngineCore`, which holds all 25 GB and survives a signal sent to its
parent's PID alone — so a teardown that kills only the parent reports the card
FREE while it is full, and on WSL2 the next tenant does not fail clean: it
spills to host shared memory and collapses throughput 10-40x while reporting
success.

Two measurements bound this, and they say different things:

  * A GPU-free stand-in (`selftest --case pgroup`) shows the shape is possible:
    the wrapper's `wait()` returns over a live grandchild, a PID-only signal
    leaks it, and only the recorded process group reaps both.
  * On the real server (2026-08-24, operator-authorised): `kill -TERM` to the
    process group reaped `VLLM::EngineCore`, and the VRAM came back at t+2s —
    six seconds BEFORE the parent exited. So on that shutdown a group signal
    ordered itself favourably and `arm run`'s release would have been correct.

The group kill below is built on the second. The VRAM gate is built on the fact
that the second measures ONE shutdown: the gate exists for the day the ordering
is different, which is exactly the day nothing else would notice.

So `arm run`'s direct child is not vLLM. It is this script's `_supervise` verb,
which:

  * puts vLLM in its own process group and records the group id,
  * on teardown kills THAT recorded group — never a pattern, never `pkill`,
  * kills before reaping, so the group id cannot be recycled out from under it
    (the leader stays an unreaped zombie, pinning its PID),
  * and does not exit until `nvidia-smi` shows the VRAM back at its
    pre-launch baseline.

Because the supervisor's exit is what releases the lease, that last step is the
whole guarantee. If the VRAM does not come back, the supervisor deliberately
stays resident holding the lease and says so, every 30 s, forever: an
over-held lease costs a queue, an under-held one corrupts the box.

`try-vision` is the guarded start (TD ruling, 2026-08-25). The vision tower
fits with the 128k window fully intact — measured, twice — but no image was ever
served end to end, because both vision boots came up unreachable. So the verb
that turns the eyes on is also the verb that proves them: it starts with the
tower, sends one image, checks the model names the colour, and on any failure
captures evidence and puts the known-good text-only server back. The operator's
chat is working when it returns, or the exit code says a human is needed.

`--uds` (on `start` and `try-vision`) serves vLLM on a Unix socket behind a TCP
proxy this launcher owns and supervises, so the consumers' port is held by a
socket that binds and listens together. It exists because every start here whose
bind->listen gap exceeded ~100 s came up with a live listener that never received
a SYN. Consumers cannot tell: `http://127.0.0.1:8001` is byte-unchanged. Off by
default — direct TCP text-only is the proven configuration, and it is what every
restore returns to.

Verbs:  start · try-vision · stop · status · selftest
        (`_supervise` and `_proxy` are internal)
"""
